Only 1 of 9 test methods is smell-free.

---

## Findings by Severity

### 🔴 HIGH

#### 1. Conditional Test Logic
**File:** `OrderProcessorTests.cs` · **Method:** `ProcessOrder_SetsCorrectStatus` (line 24)

```csharp
if (result.TotalAmount > 100)
    Assert.AreEqual("PremiumProcessed", result.Status);
else
    Assert.AreEqual("StandardProcessed", result.Status);
```

**Problem:** The `if/else` means one assertion branch never executes — you silently skip half the verification. **Risk:** Hidden test gaps; a regression in either branch may go undetected.

**Fix — split into two explicit tests:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void ProcessOrder_UnderThreshold_SetsStandardStatus()
{
    var order = new Order { Items = { new OrderItem("SKU-CHEAP", 1) } };
    var result = new OrderProcessor(_db, _email, _inventory).ProcessOrder(order);
    Assert.AreEqual("StandardProcessed", result.Status);
}

[TestMethod]
public void ProcessOrder_OverThreshold_SetsPremiumStatus()
{
    var order = new Order { Items = { new OrderItem("SKU-EXPENSIVE", 10) } };
    var result = new OrderProcessor(_db, _email, _inventory).ProcessOrder(order);
    Assert.AreEqual("PremiumProcessed", result.Status);
}
```

---

#### 2. Assertion-Free Test (a.k.a. Unknown Test)
**File:** `OrderProcessorTests.cs` · **Method:** `ProcessOrder_CompletesWithoutError` (line 43)

```csharp
processor.ProcessOrder(order);
// no assertions
```

**Problem:** Passes even if `ProcessOrder` returns garbage — the only thing it catches is an unhandled exception. **Risk:** False confidence; coverage looks good but nothing is verified.

**Fix — assert the outcome:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void ProcessOrder_SingleItem_ReturnsProcessedResult()
{
    var processor = new OrderProcessor(_db, _email, _inventory);
    var order = new Order { Items = { new OrderItem("SKU-1", 1) } };

    var result = processor.ProcessOrder(order);

    Assert.IsNotNull(result);
    Assert.AreEqual("StandardProcessed", result.Status);
}
```

---

#### 3. Sleepy Test
**File:** `OrderProcessorTests.cs` · **Method:** `ProcessOrder_AsyncNotification_IsSent` (line 91)

```csharp
Thread.Sleep(2000);
```

**Problem:** 2-second wall-clock delay every run. Too short → flaky; too long → slow suite. **Risk:** Non-deterministic failures in CI, suite slowdown.

**Fix — use a deterministic wait or make the API awaitable:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public async Task ProcessOrder_AsyncNotification_IsSent()
